{
  "answer_type": "final",
  "answer": "Pivot-based baselines involve translating from a source language to a pivot language and then from the pivot language to the target language in two separate steps. This approach is considered a strong baseline in zero-shot translation scenarios, although it is often less efficient than other methods like multilingual NMT systems that use a single encoder-decoder model for translation [E1]. An example of a pivot-based baseline is pivoting$_{\\rm m}$, which uses multilingual NMT (MNMT) to perform the translation in two steps, with each step receiving supervised signals from parallel corpora [E4].",
  "citations": [
